The dataset for this task is named edge-cases. It has exactly 20 keywords, so 190 unordered pairs, and every vector has 32 integer entries. The whole dataset is below; in the vector list, each line is one keyword and its nonzero entries as 0-based index: value, and all unlisted entries are 0. The keywords are exactly these lines' keys.
base: {0: 1000000}
base-same-direction: {0: 500000}
cos-0.70-with-base: {0: 700000, 1: 500000, 2: 500000, 3: 100000}
cos-0.80-with-base: {0: 800000, 1: 600000}
cos-0.90-with-base: {0: 900000, 1: 300000, 2: 300000, 3: 100000}
just-below-0.90-with-base: {0: 900000, 1: 300000, 2: 300000, 3: 200000}
orthogonal-to-base: {1: 1000000}
opposite-of-base: {0: -1000000}
opposite-of-base-short: {0: -500000}
third-axis: {2: 1000000}
three-four: {0: 300000, 1: 400000}
four-three: {0: 400000, 1: 300000}
three-minus-four: {0: 300000, 1: -400000}
three-four-doubled: {0: 600000, 1: 800000}
ones: {0: 100000, 1: 100000, 2: 100000, 3: 100000}
ones-doubled: {0: 200000, 1: 200000, 2: 200000, 3: 200000}
ones-one-flipped: {0: 100000, 1: 100000, 2: 100000, 3: -100000}
two-ones: {0: 100000, 1: 100000}
last-axis: {31: 100000}
zero-one-zero-one: {1: 100000, 3: 100000}

For an unordered pair of keywords (a, b), let u and v be their vectors. Define dot = sum_i u_i v_i, norm_u = sum_i u_i^2, norm_v = sum_i v_i^2. The pair qualifies for the threshold "0.90" exactly when dot >= 0 and dot^2 >= (9/10)^2 * norm_u * norm_v. Use exact integer or Fraction arithmetic, not binary floating point. Report each qualifying pair once, with a and b in lexicographic order (the smaller keyword first; all keywords are lowercase ASCII, so ordinary alphabetical order). The answer must contain every qualifying pair: min_recall = "1.0" requires all of them, and the budget of 190 does not change which pairs qualify (it only limits how many candidate pairs a scoring workflow may examine). Submit no pair that does not qualify.
